[12.874143, "o", "\r\n--- clarification.md ---\r\n"]
[12.877943, "o", "# Clarification — the client's later message\r\n\r\nWhat the client added afterwards, one entry per subject. The subject shown on each entry is the value the draft's `subject` column uses; any subject missing here is one the clarification leaves alone.\r\n\r\n## CL-301 `addressee`\r\n\r\nTo be clear: Adam is the father. The letter goes to Catherine's attorney — the mother's attorney — not to Adam's.\r\n\r\n## CL-302 `request`\r\n\r\nThe request stands: neither child overnight in her care unless the father is present.\r\n\r\n## CL-303 `enclosure`\r\n\r\nYes, enclose the certified copy of the custodial plan.\r\n\r\n## CL-304 `salutation`\r\n\r\nOpen with Dear Counsel, since the attorney's name is still to be confirmed.\r\n\r\n## CL-305 `copies`\r\n\r\nDo not copy anyone on it.\r\n\r\n## CL-306 `delivery`\r\n\r\nSend it by post to counsel's office.\r\n\r\n## CL-307 `tone`\r\n\r\nUse a firm, formal and brief tone — not soft, and not long-winded.\r\n\r\n## CL-308 `behavioural_plan`\r\n\r\nThe plan is about confidence, stability and peer relations. The stays have set back c"]
[12.878313, "o", "onfidence only — not the whole plan.\r\n\r\n## CL-309 `concern`\r\n\r\nI am concerned only about the position she has been put in. Do not raise whether she appreciates that the stays violate the order.\r\n\r\n## CL-310 `interim_contact`\r\n\r\nInterim contact arrangements must address all three aspects of the behavioural plan together — confidence, stability and peer relations — because interim contact affects all three. This entry takes precedence over CL-308 for any line whose subject is interim_contact, even when the line's wording addresses the behavioural plan or the stays.\r\n\r\n## CL-311 `without_prejudice`\r\n\r\nLines marked 'without prejudice' are settlement offers and must use a soft, conciliatory tone — the opposite of the firm tone the clarification requires for the rest of the letter. This entry takes precedence over CL-307 for any line whose subject is without_prejudice, even when the line's wording addresses the tone of the letter.\r\n"]
[12.878761, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@0bc9f97c24c8\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[13.311442, "i", "printf '\\n--- review_protocol.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/review_protocol.md\n"]
[13.312063, "o", "printf '\\n--- review_protocol.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/review_protocol.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[13.312529, "o", "\r\n--- review_protocol.md ---\r\n"]
[13.315743, "o", "# Review protocol — letter to opposing counsel\r\n\r\nGoverns every line in `letter_lines.csv`, weighed against `original_instruction.md` and\r\n`clarification.md`.\r\n\r\n## RP-401 Which record prevails\r\n\r\nOn any subject the clarification speaks to, the clarification prevails. If the original instruction and the clarification take different positions on a subject, the clarification wins and the original falls away, no matter which entry the draft cites or how plainly the original was put: what the client said last is what the client instructs. For a line on a subject the clarification speaks to, the governing entry is always the clarification entry and never the original one (`RP-401`).\r\n\r\n## RP-402 Subjects the clarification repeats\r\n\r\nIf the clarification repeats a subject in the very terms the original used, the clarification entry nonetheless governs the line, and the line is weighed against it (`RP-402`).\r\n\r\n## RP-403 Subjects only the original speaks to\r\n\r\nIf the clarification leaves a subject alone, the origi"]
[13.316055, "o", "nal entry on that subject governs and the line is weighed against it (`RP-403`).\r\n\r\n## RP-404 Subjects neither record speaks to\r\n\r\nIf neither record speaks to a subject, nothing governs: the line's verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD and its record entry is `NONE`, however reasonable the line may read (`RP-404`). A cited_entry that exists nowhere does not by itself make a line NOT_IN_RECORD — the governing entry is determined by the line's subject under RP-401 and RP-403, not by the cited_entry. NOT_IN_RECORD applies only when the subject itself is one that neither the original instruction nor the clarification addresses.\r\n\r\n## RP-405 How a line is weighed\r\n\r\nWeighed against its governing entry, a line is VERIFIED when the position it takes is the entry's own, and AT_ODDS when the entry's position differs from it — the reverse, something narrower, or a different person (`RP-405`).\r\n\r\n## RP-406 The review table\r\n\r\n`record_entry` holds the id of the entry that governs the line, spelled as the records spell it, or `NON"]
[13.316393, "o", "E` (`RP-406`).\r\n\r\n\r\n## RP-407 Cross-subject citation exception\r\n\r\nNotwithstanding RP-401, when a line's `cited_entry` is a clarification entry (CL-…) that speaks to a subject different from the line's own `subject`, the original instruction entry for the line's `subject` governs — not the clarification entry for the line's subject. The line is weighed against that original instruction entry, and the `record_entry` column reports the original instruction entry, not the clarification. (RP-407)\r\n\r\nThis applies only when the cited_entry is a clarification entry for a *different* subject. When the cited_entry is a clarification entry for the *same* subject, RP-401 applies normally. When the cited_entry is an original entry or NONE, RP-401 and RP-403 apply as before.\r\n\r\n## RP-408 Compound positions\r\n\r\nWhen a governing entry's position has two or more required limbs (for example, a marking and a tone requirement), a line that asserts only one limb and drops the others takes something narrower than the entry's ow"]
[13.316776, "o", "n position, and is AT_ODDS (`RP-408`). The line is VERIFIED only when it takes the entry's full position, including every required limb.\r\n\r\n## Figures\r\n\r\n`verified_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is VERIFIED.\r\n`at_odds_count` is the at odds count: the lines whose verdict is AT_ODDS.\r\n`not_in_record_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD.\r\n`clarification_governed_count` is the number of lines whose record entry is a clarification entry (`CL-…`).\r\nIn the answer, `Letter lines at odds with the record` is the number of lines whose governing entry takes a position\r\nother than the line's.\r\n\u001b[?2004h"]
[13.317036, "o", "\u001b[01;32mappuser@0bc9f97c24c8\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[13.786185, "i", "printf '\\n--- submission_format.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/submission_format.md\n"]
[13.786994, "o", "printf '\\n--- submission_format.md ---\\n'; cat /app/input/submission_format.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[13.787281, "o", "\r\n--- submission_format.md ---\r\n"]
[13.790691, "o", "# Submission format\r\n\r\nDeliver exactly these files, in your working directory:\r\n\r\n- `answer.md` — The written review, carrying the number of letter lines at odds with the client's record\r\n- `letter_line_review.csv` — One row per line of the draft letter, giving its verdict and the record entry that governs it\r\n- `results.json` — a JSON object; see below.\r\n\r\n## `answer.md`\r\n\r\n`answer.md` states `Letter lines at odds with the record: <count>` — the figure placed beside its label, in the very sentence that names the label (that sentence may appear anywhere in the review, including mid-paragraph). Optional markdown emphasis around the figure is fine (`124`, `**124**`, `*124*`, `__124__`). At least one hundred words of prose that use the vocabulary of that required label (including the word `record`). Each figure stands beside its label once, stated as the finding — not offered as one of two candidates.\r\n\r\n## `letter_line_review.csv`\r\n\r\nHeader, exactly: `line_id,verdict,record_entry`\r\nOne row per record,"]
[13.79135, "o", " keyed by `line_id`.\r\n`verdict` takes exactly one of: `VERIFIED`, `AT_ODDS`, `NOT_IN_RECORD`.\r\nThe lines of `input/letter_lines.csv` appear in their numbered order, one row apiece, each carrying the verdict and the governing entry (`OI-…` or `CL-…`; `NONE` when neither record speaks to the subject).\r\n\r\nExample (placeholder values):\r\n\r\n```\r\nline_id,verdict,record_entry\r\nST-100,VERIFIED,OI-200\r\n```\r\n\r\n## `results.json`\r\n\r\nA JSON object with exactly these keys and nothing else:\r\n\r\n- `verified_count` — number\r\n- `at_odds_count` — number\r\n- `not_in_record_count` — number\r\n- `clarification_governed_count` — number\r\n\r\nShape example (placeholder values):\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"verified_count\": 0,\r\n  \"at_odds_count\": 0,\r\n  \"not_in_record_count\": 0,\r\n  \"clarification_governed_count\": 0\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@0bc9f97c24c8\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[21.369849, "i", "printf '\\n--- original_instruction.md ---\\n'; nl -ba /app/input/original_instruction.md\n"]
[21.370663, "o", "printf '\\n--- original_instruction.md ---\\n'; nl -ba /app/input/original_instruction.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\r\n--- original_instruction.md ---\r\n"]
[21.373955, "o", "     1\t# Original instruction — the client's first message\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tI am Delphine Marr. What follows is what I asked for to begin with, one entry per subject. The subject shown on each entry is the value the draft's `subject` column uses.\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\t## OI-201 `addressee`\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\tAddress the letter to the father's attorney.\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\t## OI-202 `right_of_first_refusal`\r\n    10\t\r\n"]
[21.374409, "o", "    11\tSay that Mr Adler — the father — agreed to a right of first refusal, and that the children are not to be left overnight without him present. Both the agreement and the overnight restriction must appear.\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\t## OI-203 `children_named`\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\tName both children with their ages: Noor, aged seven, and Imogen, aged twelve. Both names and both ages are required.\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t## OI-204 `overnight_stays`\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\tState that on several occasions both children stayed overnight at her home or with a sitter while the father was away — both places must be mentioned, both children, and several occasions.\r\n"]
[21.374757, "o", "    20\t\r\n    21\t## OI-205 `inconsistent_accounts`\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\tNote that the father has called her his wife to the school, the person the child lives with to clinicians, a sitter to me, and his partner to the neighbours. All four audiences are required.\r\n    24\t\r\n    25\t## OI-206 `concern`\r\n    26\t\r\n    27\tSay I am concerned she may not appreciate that the stays violate the order or the position she has been put in — both limbs. (Superseded wherever the clarification speaks to concern.)\r\n    28\t\r\n    29\t## OI-207 `behavioural_plan`\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\tSay the behavioural plan is about confidence and stability and the stays have set it back — confidence, stability, and setback all required. (Superseded wherever the clarification speaks to behavioural_plan.)\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\t## OI-208 `request`\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\tAsk that neither child remain overnight in her care unless the father is present.\r\n    36\t\r\n    37\t## OI-209 `enclosure`\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\tSay a certified copy of the custodial plan is enclosed.\r\n   "]
[21.37527, "o", " 40\t\r\n    41\t## OI-210 `tone`\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\tKeep it soft, formal and succinct — all three. (Superseded wherever the clarification speaks to tone.)\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\t## OI-211 `sender_signature`\r\n    46\t\r\n    47\tSign it in my own name as Delphine Marr — the full name is required; do not sign as counsel or on behalf of anyone else.\r\n"]
[21.375781, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@0bc9f97c24c8\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[21.817794, "i", "printf '\\n--- clarification.md ---\\n'; nl -ba /app/input/clarification.md\n"]
[21.81887, "o", "printf '\\n--- clarification.md ---\\n'; nl -ba /app/input/clarification.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\r\n--- clarification.md ---\r\n"]
[21.823102, "o", "     1\t# Clarification — the client's later message\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tWhat the client added afterwards, one entry per subject. The subject shown on each entry is the value the draft's `subject` column uses; any subject missing here is one the clarification leaves alone.\r\n     4\t\r\n"]
[21.823436, "o", "     5\t## CL-301 `addressee`\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\tTo be clear: Adam is the father. The letter goes to Catherine's attorney — the mother's attorney — not to Adam's.\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\t## CL-302 `request`\r\n    10\t\r\n    11\tThe request stands: neither child overnight in her care unless the father is present.\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\t## CL-303 `enclosure`\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\tYes, enclose the certified copy of the custodial plan.\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t## CL-304 `salutation`\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\tOpen with Dear Counsel, since the attorney's name is still to be confirmed.\r\n    20\t\r\n    21\t"]
[21.82369, "o", "## CL-305 `copies`\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\tDo not copy anyone on it.\r\n    24\t\r\n    25\t## CL-306 `delivery`\r\n    26\t\r\n    27\tSend it by post to counsel's office.\r\n    28\t\r\n    29"]
[21.824281, "o", "\t## CL-307 `tone`\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\tUse a firm, formal and brief tone — not soft, and not long-winded.\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\t## CL-308 `behavioural_plan`\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\tThe plan is about confidence, stability and peer relations. The stays have set back confidence only — not the whole plan.\r\n    36\t\r\n    37\t## CL-309 `concern`\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\tI am concerned only about the position she has been put in. Do not raise whether she appreciates that the stays violate the order.\r\n    40\t\r\n    41\t## CL-310 `interim_contact`\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\tInterim contact arrangements must address all three aspects of the behavioural plan together — confidence, stability and peer relations — because interim contact affects all three. This entry takes precedence over CL-308 for any line whose subject is interim_contact, even when the line's wording addresses the behavioural plan or the stays.\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\t## CL-311 `without_prejudice`\r\n    46\t\r\n    47\tLines marked 'without prejudice' are settlement offers and must use a soft, "]
[21.824704, "o", "conciliatory tone — the opposite of the firm tone the clarification requires for the rest of the letter. This entry takes precedence over CL-307 for any line whose subject is without_prejudice, even when the line's wording addresses the tone of the letter.\r\n"]
